course: dictionary
lesson: 3362
title: Words for big feelings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Excitement is a strong, happy feeling. You get it when you look forward to something fun, or when something surprising is happening. «She was full of excitement before her birthday party.»
explain: Excitement can also mean the busy, fun activity itself. «The excitement of the fair» means all the rides, noise and games that make people feel excited.
explain: Excitement lives in your body too. It can make your heart beat fast, your tummy flutter and make it hard to sit still or sleep, a bit like being nervous. Notice it, and calm down when you need to, for example by slowing your breathing.
fact: Excitement is a noun, so you can have excitement, or be full of excitement.
fact: «Excitement» means a strong, happy feeling about something fun or surprising that is coming up or happening now.
fact: «Excitement» can also mean the fun, busy activity itself, like «the excitement of a fair».
fact: «Jump with excitement» means jump because you feel very happy and thrilled.
fact: «In all the excitement» means something was forgotten because everyone was busy and rushing about.
exercise: «Toad cries: travel, change, excitement, a new place every day!» What does excitement mean here? | Losing your way | Feeling sleepy after a long trip | Being scared of the road | *Strong, happy feelings about new adventures
exercise: Dad says, «In all the excitement, I forgot my lunch.» What does «in all the excitement» suggest? | *Everyone was busy and rushing about because of something fun | He had no money for lunch | He was bored and had nothing to do | He did not like lunch
exercise: Which of these is a sign of excitement in your body? | You yawn and feel sleepy | You sit very still and calm | *Your heart beats fast and your tummy flutters | You feel cold and shiver
test: Which spelling is correct for the strong, happy feeling? | excitemant | exitement | excitment | *excitement
test: Which sentence uses excitement to mean the fun activity? | The film made us cry because it was so sad. | *We went to the fair, and the excitement of the rides kept us busy all day. | We sat quietly in the library for an hour. | The wet, grey morning made us feel tired.
exam: «She jumped with excitement when she saw the puppy.» What does «jumped with excitement» mean? | She jumped over a wall to escape | *She jumped up and down because she felt very happy and thrilled | She fell down in a fright | She jumped into cold water
